From ffaeee989523b5a9e404ec3ed00c8d37b9cfc278 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Metacraft Labs <dev@metacraft-labs.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 07:59:50 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] test(vm2): expose the archive tree through the reactor, so a
 block can be sealed

M14 extended `world_state_reference::MemoryMerkleDB` with the archive tree --
the fifth WorldState tree, the append-only tree of block-header hashes that
every block advances. That extension is a self-contained C++ change and it does
not reach `avm.wasm`: the AVM adapter in vm2 wraps the reference DB privately
and exposes only `LowLevelMerkleDBInterface`, which has fourteen methods and no
archive among them, because the AVM never reads one.

This overlay carries the archive across that adapter and out through the
reactor, and nothing else.

  * `bb::avm2::simulation::MemoryMerkleDB` gains two NON-VIRTUAL methods,
    `update_archive` and `get_archive_snapshot`. They are not added to
    `LowLevelMerkleDBInterface`: the interface's fourteen stay fourteen, so
    nothing the AVM sees moves and every count pinned against that interface is
    untouched.

  * `update_archive` takes the block's state reference as the AVM's own
    `TreeSnapshots` rather than the reference DB's `world_state::TreeRoots`.
    `TreeSnapshots` is msgpack-declared (`MSGPACK_CAMEL_CASE_FIELDS`) and
    already crosses this boundary; `TreeRoots` has no msgpack declaration at
    all. The `TreeRoots` the adapter builds from it leaves the archive field at
    its default, which is never read -- `state_reference_equals` compares the
    four StateReference trees and ignores the fifth, which is why M14 wrote it.

  * TWO reactor exports, not one. Appending a header is half of it: the next
    header's `lastArchive` is a READ of the archive, so a module that can only
    append gives a caller no way to anchor block N+1 against block N. The read
    is what a seal cannot do without.

The reactor's export list goes from forty-nine names to fifty-one. That is a
different artefact from M12's and M13's and is measured as one.
